Short:        Ogg/MP3/WAV/FLAC/MOD to AIFF/8SVX, through datatypes
Author:       AmiCradle Team
Uploader:     AmiCradle Team
Type:         mus/misc
Version:      1.0
Requires:     AmigaOS 3.0+, 68040 + FPU, GFOpenDatatypes (gfopenaiff,
              gfopenwave, gfopensound 47.2, gfopenmodule 47.4)
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0

OpenAmigaAudioTools turns sounds and music into what an Amiga plays with
no decoding: 16-bit big-endian AIFF (the default), IFF 8SVX for Paula,
WAV, or raw PCM.

  C/ogg2iff   C/mp32iff   C/wav2iff   C/flac2iff   C/mod2iff   C/x2iff

They are one program under six names. Every read and every save goes
through datatypes:

  - A sound opens with NewDTObject, so whatever sound datatypes you have
    installed are inputs too. It is read a piece at a time with
    OIM_READSOUND where the datatype offers it.
  - The result is saved by gfopenaiff.datatype or gfopenwave.datatype
    (AIFF, WAV, raw PCM), or by sound.datatype (8SVX), with DTM_WRITE.
  - --verify opens each result again through the datatypes.

  x2iff -r 22050 Music:theme.ogg TO RAM:theme.aiff
  mod2iff -f 8svx -m -r 11025 Mods/ -o Sfx/
  x2iff --info Music:theme.ogg

Options: -f aiff|8svx|wav|raw, -o, -r rate, -m mono, -b 8|16 (TPDF
dither on 8-bit), -e big|little, -u, -n[=dB] normalise, -g dB, -s/-t/-l
seconds, -T[=dB] trim silence, -q 0|1|2, -i, -v, --interp, --verify.
"x2iff -h" lists them all.

A datatype keeps the result in memory until it saves it: allow about
5 MB a minute of 22 kHz stereo 16-bit audio. Ogg Vorbis and MP3 are
decoded in soft float by gfopensound.datatype, slowly; FLAC and modules
are quick. On a PC the same tools for Linux carry their own decoders
(openamigaaudiotools-1.0-linux-*.tar.gz).

Not yet run on an Amiga: built and checked on the PC.

Source: https://github.com/DalsinAI/openamigaaudiotools
